<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch8-section-mid-latitude-synoptic-scale-analysis-subsection-hydrostatic-balance" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Hydrostatic balance</title>

    <p>
        The two largest terms in the vertical equation of motion are the gravity acceleration term and the pressure gradient acceleration term.
        All other terms are at least two orders of magnitude smaller
        (i.e., at least 100 times smaller).
    </p>

    <p>
        Neglecting all but these two terms in the vertical equation of motion produces
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            0 = -\frac{1}{\rho} \frac{\partial p}{\partial z} - g
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        which can be rearranged into the familiar formula for hydrostatic balance:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            \frac{\partial p}{\partial z} = -\rho g
        </md>
    </p>

</subsection>